1.what is cloud computing?
Cloud computing means getting computing resources such as:
.Servers
.Storage
.Database
.Networking
.Security
.Monitoring
.Analytics
.Application services etc
over the internet without purchasing and maintaining all the physical
infrastructure yourself.

Tradtional infrastructure
Company purchases:

servers -> Storage -> Network -> Data center -> Power -> Cooling -> Maintenance

Problems:
.High upfront cost
.Hardware procurement takes time
.Limited scalability
.Hardware maintenance
.capacity planning
.Disaster recovery is exepensive

Cloud infrastructure
Instead of purchasing a physical server, you can provision a virtaul server in AWS in 
minutes.
Example:
Traditional:
Buy server --> wait --> Install OS --> configure network --> deploy app 

AWS:
Create EC2 --> configure security --> deploy app

2.Cloud Service Models
IaaS - Infrastructure as a Service
AWS manages physical infrastructure

You manage:
.OS
.Applications
.Runtime
.Data

Examples:
.Amazon EC2

PaaS-Platform as a Service
AWS manages more of the underlying infrastructure/platform

Examples:
.AWS Elastic Beanstalk
.AWS App Runner

SaaS-Software as a service
Complete application delivered to users (ready made to use app):

Examples:
.Gmail
.Microsoft 365

Simple Understanding:
---------------------
IaaS -> Infrastructure
PaaS -> Platform
SaaS -> Software

3.AWS Global Infrastrucure:
Region
A geographical AWS location.

Examples:
ap-south-1  mumbai
ap-south-2  hyderabad
us-east-1   N.Virginia
us-west-2   Oregon

A Region contains multiple Availability zones

Availabilty Zone
An AZ is one or more discrete data centers with independent infrastructure

Example:
Mumbai Region
|
|---AZ-A
|--AZ-B
|--AZ-C

Why multiple AZs?

High Availability.

Never design an important production application around a single AZ when the workload requires refional
resilence

Edge locations
Used primarily for aws edge services such as Cloudfront
Architecure:
Users
|
Cloudfront Edge
|
AWS Origin

